# Walking through a proof

```elixir
Mix.install([{:truestamp_verifier, path: __DIR__}])
```

## What this notebook does

This notebook runs the reference verifier in this directory over one Truestamp proof and
shows what each step of `SPEC.md` section 10.2 found. It prints every value the trace of
section 10.6 holds: for each DERIVE row whose part is a CHECKED value (section 3.7), other
than a row of LINK_HEAD, LINK_KEY_EVENT or LINK_CHAIN_ORDER, the declared value beside the
recomputed one. A few DERIVE rows compare values the trace does not hold; the section on
step 9 names them.

It computes nothing itself: it hashes nothing, verifies no signature, walks no Merkle path
and decodes no file. Every value it prints comes from the verifier: the report of
`SPEC.md` section 10.5 and the trace of section 10.6. The cells only choose a proof, run the
verifier and print what it gives. A test in `test/walkthrough_test.exs` runs every cell but
the first, whose `Mix.install` its own project already satisfies. It fails if a cell hashes,
signs, verifies or decodes anything of its own, or names a module that only the tests have.

The run is offline, as every run of the reference verifier is (section 8.3). It reads no
chain and no source, so it establishes no submission window. The section on steps 10 to 12
says what that leaves open.

To open it, start Livebook with `livebook server` and open `elixir/walkthrough.livemd` from
a checkout of this repository, or open the file in the Livebook desktop app. The first cell
installs the verifier from this directory. The first time, it fetches the verifier's one
dependency, the Truestamp Merkle library, from GitHub.

## Configuration

This cell picks the proof, and the configuration the verifier runs it under as
`Truestamp.Verifier.Config` takes it. Step 0 of `SPEC.md` section 10.2, CONFIGURATION,
fixes E_v, the network pins and the keyring before the file is read.

The default is the sound item proof `vectors/positive/item.proof.json`. It was signed under
the `test` environment by the key in `vectors/keyring.test.json`, so the run supplies that
keyring (section 8.3), exactly as the conformance case `sound-item` does.

To walk through another proof, change `proof_file`. Any file under `vectors/positive/`
runs under this configuration: `beacon`, `beacon-empty`, `block`, `block-rotation`,
`block-emergency-rotation`, `entropy_bitcoin`, `entropy_nist`, `entropy_stellar`, `item`,
`item-genesis-witness` and `item-rotation-witness`, each `<name>.proof.json`. Other
configurations:

* keyless mode, where the signature is checked only against the key the proof carries:
  `keyring: :none`. The label is then `unattributed` unless a row fails (section 8.3);
* a proof from Truestamp's `dev` or `staging` environment: `environment: "dev"` or
  `environment: "staging"`, with a keyring that holds its key, `keyring: {:file, path}`,
  or keyless mode, `keyring: :none`. A run whose environment is not `prod` needs one of
  the two (sections 6.4 and 8.3);
* a production proof: `Truestamp.Verifier.Config.new!()`, whose environment is `prod`
  and whose keyring is the compiled pin, `spec/keyring.production.json`. That file holds
  no key yet, so every production proof fails KEY_BINDING with `key_binding_failed`
  under it. Until it holds one, walk a production proof with a keyring that holds its key,
  `Truestamp.Verifier.Config.new!(keyring: {:file, path})`, or in keyless mode,
  `Truestamp.Verifier.Config.new!(keyring: :none)`;
* a hash to compare with the proof's claims (step 13): add
  `expected_hash: "<lowercase hex>"`.

```elixir
repository = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
proof_file = Path.join(repository, "vectors/positive/item.proof.json")

config =
  Truestamp.Verifier.Config.new!(
    environment: "test",
    keyring: {:file, Path.join(repository, "vectors/keyring.test.json")}
  )
```

## Printing helpers

These functions only print what the verifier gives. `print_rows` prints report rows as
aligned columns: step, status, reason code (`-` for a pass, which has none) and part, the
JSON Pointer of the member the row is about (`null` for a row about no single member).
`print_trace_row` prints one trace row with its declared value above its recomputed value.
`print_label` prints a report's label, its code, E_v (section 6.4 asks for a non-production
E_v beside every verdict) and, in keyless mode, the words section 8.3 asks for beside every
verdict. `no_derive` says which step ended a run whose trace is empty. Such a run always
has a failed row: a run that passes steps 4 to 7 reaches DERIVE, which either gives the
proof's `/block/block_hash` a trace row or fails with a fault (`internal_error`).

```elixir
value_text = fn
  nil -> "null"
  value when is_binary(value) -> value
  value -> inspect(value)
end

print_rows = fn rows ->
  code_width = rows |> Enum.map(&String.length(&1["code"] || "-")) |> Enum.max(fn -> 4 end)

  columns = fn step, status, code, part ->
    IO.puts(
      String.pad_trailing(step, 15) <>
        String.pad_trailing(status, 7) <>
        String.pad_trailing(code, max(code_width, 4) + 2) <> part
    )
  end

  columns.("step", "status", "code", "part")

  Enum.each(rows, fn row ->
    columns.(row["step"], row["status"], row["code"] || "-", value_text.(row["part"]))
  end)
end

rows_of = fn report, steps -> Enum.filter(report["rows"], &(&1["step"] in steps)) end

# Where SPEC.md defines each construction's check, beside the row of section 7.3 it names.
spec_section = %{
  "claims_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "item_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "entropy_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "observation_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "block_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (b)",
  "genesis" => "10.2 step 9 (b)",
  "key_event" => "10.3, key events",
  "handover" => "10.3, key events",
  "epoch_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (c)",
  "bitcoin" => "10.2 step 9 (d)",
  "inclusion" => "10.3, LINK_INCLUSION",
  "witnesses" => "10.3, LINK_WITNESS"
}

print_trace_row = fn row, number ->
  section = Map.get(spec_section, row["construction"], "10.2 step 9")

  IO.puts(
    String.pad_leading("#{number}", 3) <>
      "  " <>
      String.pad_trailing(row["construction"], 18) <>
      String.pad_trailing(row["status"], 6) <> "SPEC.md #{section}"
  )

  IO.puts("    part        #{row["part"]}")
  IO.puts("    declared    #{value_text.(row["declared"])}")
  IO.puts("    recomputed  #{value_text.(row["recomputed"])}")
  IO.puts("    inputs      #{Enum.join(row["inputs"], ", ")}")
end

print_label = fn report ->
  IO.puts("Label        #{report["label"]}")
  if report["code"], do: IO.puts("Code         #{report["code"]}")
  IO.puts("Environment  #{report["environment"]}")

  if report["trust_root"]["kind"] == "none" do
    IO.puts(
      "Keyless      the signature was checked only against the key the document " <>
        "carries (SPEC.md 8.3)"
    )
  end
end

no_derive = fn report ->
  %{"step" => step, "code" => code} = Enum.find(report["rows"], &(&1["status"] == "fail"))

  "This run ended at #{step} with #{code} (SPEC.md 10.2), before DERIVE compared any " <>
    "declared value, so the trace is empty: there is no DERIVE value to show or change."
end

:ok
```

## Run the verifier

`Truestamp.Verifier.run_file/2` makes steps 0 to 14 over the file. A run that passes steps
0 to 3 gives a run, and `Truestamp.Verifier.Report.report/1` writes it as the report of
section 10.5, exactly what `mix truestamp.verify` prints. Anything else gives no report,
and this cell stops with a message that says which:

* a failure at steps 0 to 3 is a bare rejection (section 10.2, the gate);
* a proof or keyring file that cannot be read is an input error outside verification
  (section 12.2, exit code 1), not a step's failure.

The verifier's third answer with no report, `{:fault, description}`, is a fault inside it
before steps 0 to 3 passed (sections 10.5 and 12.2). Only a bug in the verifier gives one,
so the cell has no clause for it, and such a bug stops the cell with the fault unmatched.

```elixir
run =
  case Truestamp.Verifier.run_file(config, proof_file) do
    {:ok, run} ->
      run

    {:error, {:unreadable, file, reason}} ->
      raise "#{file} cannot be read (#{inspect(reason)}). That is an input error outside " <>
              "verification, which gives no report (SPEC.md 12.2, exit code 1). Check the " <>
              "paths in the configuration cell."

    {:rejected, rejection} ->
      why = if rejection.sub_reason, do: " (#{rejection.sub_reason})", else: ""

      raise "The run of #{proof_file} is a bare rejection: #{rejection.code}#{why}. " <>
              "#{rejection.message} A failure at steps 0 to 3 gives no report " <>
              "(SPEC.md 10.2, the gate)."
  end

report = Truestamp.Verifier.Report.report(run)

IO.puts("Proof        #{Path.relative_to(proof_file, repository)}")
IO.puts("Type         #{value_text.(report["type"])}")
IO.puts("Subject id   #{value_text.(report["subject_id"])}")
IO.puts("Rows         #{length(report["rows"])}")
IO.puts("Trace rows   #{length(run.trace)}")
print_label.(report)

:ok
```

## Steps 0 to 6: the gate

`SPEC.md` section 10.2 calls steps 0 to 6 the gate. Nothing in the proof but its
`environment` member is interpreted until the key is bound and the signature verified.

* **0 CONFIGURATION** fixes E_v, the network pins and the keyring that governs
  (sections 6.4, 8.3 and 9).
* **1 READ** and **2 PARSE** apply the parsing rules J1 to J6 (section 10.1).
* **3 ENVELOPE** checks that the file is exactly the envelope of section 3.1, and that the
  bytes of the key K pass the key checks of section 6.2.
* **4 ENVIRONMENT** compares the proof's `environment` with E_v (section 6.4).
* **5 KEY_BINDING** looks for K's 32 octets in the keyring that governs (sections 6.1 and
  8.3).
* **6 SIGNATURE** verifies the signature V under K over `proof_hash`, the SHA-256 of 0x50
  and JCS of the proof (sections 6.1 and 6.2). The verifier computed that hash; the
  notebook only shows its row.

A failure at steps 4 to 6 ends the run: every later step up to EXPECTED_HASH is one row,
`skip prior_failure`, with part null. The trust root and attribution say whose key signed
the proof, as far as this run can tell (section 10.5).

```elixir
gate = ~w(CONFIGURATION READ PARSE ENVELOPE ENVIRONMENT KEY_BINDING SIGNATURE)
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, gate))

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Trust root   #{report["trust_root"]["kind"]}")
IO.puts("  digest     #{value_text.(report["trust_root"]["digest"])}")
IO.puts("  location   #{value_text.(report["trust_root"]["location"])}")
IO.puts("Attribution  #{report["attribution"]}")
```

## Steps 7 and 8: SHAPE and POLICY

**7 SHAPE** (`SPEC.md` section 10.2, step 7) checks `format` and `version`, the JSON Schema
`spec/schema.json`, the checks the schema cannot make, and `constructions`. When it passes,
the report takes the proof's type and subject id. A failure here ends the run as a failure
at the gate does.

**8 POLICY** (step 8) compares every `network` member with the verifier's pins (section 9).
Each comparison is one row.

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(SHAPE POLICY)))
```

## Step 9: DERIVE, and the values its trace holds

DERIVE (`SPEC.md` section 10.2, step 9) recomputes every value the constructions text of
section 7.3 defines from the preimage the proof carries, compares it with the value the
proof declares for it, and checks every link of section 10.3. A proof declares and a
verifier verifies (section 10.6): each hash below was recomputed by the verifier, and the
declared value was only compared with it.

The trace of section 10.6 has one row for each DERIVE row whose part is a CHECKED value,
other than the rows of LINK_HEAD, LINK_KEY_EVENT and LINK_CHAIN_ORDER. Each trace row names
its construction (a row of section 7.3), its part, the declared value, the recomputed value,
the inputs the check read, and its status. This cell prints every value the trace holds.
`mix truestamp.verify FILE --trace TRACE` writes the same rows to TRACE, and
`vectors/positive/<name>.trace.json` holds them for each sound proof.

`recomputed` holds what the check compares `declared` with, as its table defines it
(section 10.6). For example:

* the recomputed hash, for a hash;
* the root the walk reached, for an inclusion proof's root, or null when the walk gives no
  root;
* the record's `leaf_count`, for a `tree_size`;
* 0, for a genesis `height`;
* the `height` and `previous_block_hash` of the block record that carries a rotation or an
  emergency rotation, for the key event's own `height` and `previous_block_hash`;
* the carried `active_key_id`, for the `signing_key_id` of a record that carries a
  prerotation;
* the header's nTime, formatted as a TIME, for a Bitcoin `header_time`;
* for a `handover_signature`, the hex of the 32 octets the signature was verified over.

```elixir
if run.trace == [] do
  IO.puts(no_derive.(report))
else
  run.trace
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.each(fn {row, number} -> print_trace_row.(row, number) end)
end
```

These DERIVE rows have no trace row:

* LINK_HEAD, LINK_KEY_EVENT and LINK_CHAIN_ORDER (section 10.3). LINK_KEY_EVENT is
  `info not_attributable` in a run not attributed to Truestamp (section 10.3, the
  key-history rows).
* For each Bitcoin entry, the two checks of well-formedness of step 9 (d), at
  `raw_transaction` and at `txoutproof`, which compare no values.
* For each Bitcoin entry, the two comparisons of step 9 (d) at the same two parts: the 32
  bytes the transaction's OP_RETURN pushes, against the recomputed `epoch_hash`, and the
  one hash the `txoutproof` matches, against the double SHA-256 of `raw_transaction`.
  Each compares a declared value, the 32 bytes read out of `raw_transaction` or
  `txoutproof`, with a recomputed one. Their parts are PREIMAGE (section 3.7), so they have
  no trace row, and the report gives their declared and recomputed values only when they
  fail (section 10.5); a passing row holds neither. At each of the two parts the
  well-formedness row comes first and the comparison second, in the order of the table of
  step 9 (d).
* The check of a key's bytes at each key event's `public_key` (section 10.3, key events),
  whose part is PREIMAGE too.

Here is every DERIVE row, in the report's order:

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(DERIVE)))
```

## Steps 10 to 12: what an offline run does not establish

**10 CHAIN** and **11 SOURCES** read public chains and entropy sources (section 10.4).
The reference verifier makes no such read (section 8.3), so the external gate of section
10.2 is always closed. After a failure at steps 4 to 7, CHAIN and SOURCES are each one row,
`skip prior_failure`, with part null, as every later step is (section 10.2).
Otherwise each CHAIN and SOURCES row is `skip prior_failure` when a row before CHAIN failed,
and `skip offline` when none did. When the gate opened, SHAPE passed and the proof carries
`signing_key_event`, CHAIN ends with KEY_EVENT_COMMITMENT (section 10.3), a row at
`/signing_key_event`, skipped in the same way.

**12 WINDOW** draws the submission window from the reads of this run only (section 11). No
time the proof carries is an edge: not `header_time`, not an entropy payload's time, not
the time inside an id, and not `claims.timestamp`. After a failed row, WINDOW is `skip
prior_failure` (section 11.3, rule 1). Otherwise, with no read, no candidate exists, so
WINDOW gives `info window_not_established` (section 11.3). Either way the report's
`submitted_after` and `submitted_before` are null, and the run establishes no submission
window: neither the submitted-after edge nor the submitted-before edge. That is not a
failure (section 11.3, "Absence never fails"); it is why the best label an offline run gives
a sound production proof is `internally-consistent` (sections 8.3 and 10.5). A verifier that
makes the reads, such as Truestamp's CLI, can draw both edges, but a block proof, a beacon
whose `observations` is empty and an item with no entropy witness have no submitted-after
edge (section 11.2).

When WINDOW is not skipped, its row lists, for each edge that does not exist, the rows that
could have given it a candidate, or that none could.

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES WINDOW)))

window_row = Enum.find(report["rows"], &(&1["step"] == "WINDOW"))
absent = window_row["values"]["absent"] || %{}

IO.puts("")

Enum.each(~w(submitted_after submitted_before), fn edge ->
  IO.puts("#{edge}: #{value_text.(report[edge])}")

  case Map.get(absent, edge) do
    nil ->
      :ok

    [] ->
      IO.puts("  no row could give it: the proof carries nothing that could (SPEC.md 11.2)")

    reasons ->
      Enum.each(reasons, &IO.puts("  #{&1["code"]} at #{&1["part"]}"))
  end
end)

IO.puts("window: #{report["window"]}")
```

## Steps 13 and 14: EXPECTED_HASH and VERDICT

**13 EXPECTED_HASH** compares a hash the caller gives with the proof's `claims.hash`
(section 10.2, step 13). Without one, its row is `skip expected_hash_not_checked`, with
part null. After a failure at the gate or at SHAPE, it is `skip prior_failure`, as every
later step is.

**14 VERDICT** gives the label, the attribution and the window, and adds no row (section
10.5). The label is the first of section 10.5's table that applies, and its meaning below
is that table's own words. E_v is shown beside it (section 6.4).

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(EXPECTED_HASH)))

meaning = %{
  "failed" => "a check failed, and the row says which",
  "unattributed" =>
    "the proof is intact under the key it carries, which was checked against no keyring, " <>
      "so anyone could have made it (section 8.3)",
  "supplied-keyring" =>
    "the proof is intact and signed by a key in the keyring the operator supplied; " <>
      "this says nothing about Truestamp",
  "internally-consistent" =>
    "the proof is intact and signed by Truestamp, but no read in this run established " <>
      "its submitted-before edge",
  "verified" =>
    "the proof is intact, signed by Truestamp, and places its subject's submission " <>
      "before an instant read from a public chain in this run"
}

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Label        #{report["label"]}: #{meaning[report["label"]]} (SPEC.md 10.5)")
IO.puts("Environment  #{report["environment"]}")
IO.puts("Attribution  #{report["attribution"]}")
IO.puts("Verified     #{report["verified"]}")
IO.puts("Window       #{report["window"]}")
if report["code"], do: IO.puts("Code         #{report["code"]}")

if report["trust_root"]["kind"] == "none" do
  IO.puts(
    "Keyless      the signature was checked only against the key the document " <>
      "carries (SPEC.md 8.3)"
  )
end

:ok
```

## Change one declared value

This section changes one value the proof declares, in memory, and runs the verifier on the
result. That run never reaches DERIVE. The signature covers every member of the proof
(section 6.1), so a change to any member fails SIGNATURE, unless it changes `environment`,
which ENVIRONMENT (step 4) checks before SIGNATURE (step 6). The edit here changes a 64-hex
trace value, never `environment`, so its run fails SIGNATURE, and a failure at the gate
ends the run before anything past `environment` is interpreted (section 10.2, the gate). A
changed declared value can only reach DERIVE in a proof signed again after the change, and
this notebook signs nothing. So the last cell runs the verifier on such a proof from
`vectors/negative/`, which the generator changed and then signed again.

The next cell picks the value to change, and changes its last digit. For an item, a block,
a beacon or an `entropy_nist` proof, it picks by default the part whose value the last
cell's proof changes: `/subject/item_hash`, `/block/block_hash`,
`/observations/entropy_nist/observation_hash` or `/subject/observation_hash`. It does so
when the proof's trace has that row, which a beacon whose `observations` is empty does not.
Otherwise it picks the first trace row whose declared value is 64 lowercase hex digits,
`/block/block_hash` in every sound proof here, and the last cell's proof then changes
another value. Either way, the failing row the last cell prints is the generator's change,
not this cell's edit: the generator changed the sound proof in its own way and signed the
result again.

To change another value, set `edited_part` to the part of a trace row above whose declared
value is 64 lowercase hex digits, such as `/subject/item_hash` in an item proof or
`/block/block_hash` in any other. The cell changes only such
values, and refuses any other part with a message that says so.

```elixir
# nil picks the part the last cell's proof changes, where the trace has it, and otherwise
# the first trace row whose declared value is 64 lowercase hex digits.
edited_part = nil

# For each type that has one, the declared-value vector the last cell runs and the part
# whose value it changes. Any other type takes the item's vector.
declared_vectors = %{
  "item" => {"declared-item_hash", "/subject/item_hash"},
  "block" => {"declared-block_hash-block", "/block/block_hash"},
  "beacon" =>
    {"declared-observation_hash-observation-nist", "/observations/entropy_nist/observation_hash"},
  "entropy_nist" => {"declared-observation_hash-subject", "/subject/observation_hash"}
}

{vector_name, vector_part} = Map.get(declared_vectors, report["type"], declared_vectors["item"])

hex64? = &(is_binary(&1) and &1 =~ ~r/\A[0-9a-f]{64}\z/)
hex_parts = for row <- run.trace, hex64?.(row["declared"]), do: row["part"]

edited_part =
  cond do
    run.trace == [] ->
      IO.puts(no_derive.(report))
      nil

    edited_part in hex_parts ->
      edited_part

    edited_part != nil ->
      raise ArgumentError,
            "edited_part must be the part of a trace row above whose declared value is 64 " <>
              "lowercase hex digits, and #{inspect(edited_part)} is not"

    vector_part in hex_parts ->
      vector_part

    true ->
      List.first(hex_parts)
  end

edited_document =
  if edited_part do
    segments = edited_part |> String.split("/") |> tl()
    original_value = get_in(run.proof, segments)
    last_digit = if String.ends_with?(original_value, "0"), do: "1", else: "0"
    edited_value = String.slice(original_value, 0..-2//1) <> last_digit

    IO.puts("Changed  #{edited_part}")
    IO.puts("  from   #{original_value}")
    IO.puts("  to     #{edited_value}")

    put_in(run.document, ["proof" | segments], edited_value)
  end

:ok
```

No CHECKED value (section 3.7) sits in an array or under a member name that holds `/` or
`~`, so each segment of a trace part is a member name as it stands, and `get_in` and
`put_in` follow it.

### The edited proof, as a verifier meets it

This cell runs the verifier, under the configuration above, on the edited document.
`Truestamp.Verifier.CanonicalForm.encode/1` writes it as a document's bytes; formatting
does not matter to a verifier (section 7.3, the `parsing` row). The steps before SIGNATURE
give the rows they gave above, SIGNATURE fails with `signature_invalid`, and every later
step up to EXPECTED_HASH is one row, `skip prior_failure`, with part null. DERIVE never
reads the edited value.

```elixir
signature_report =
  if edited_document do
    {:ok, signature_run} =
      Truestamp.Verifier.run(config, Truestamp.Verifier.CanonicalForm.encode(edited_document))

    Truestamp.Verifier.Report.report(signature_run)
  end

if signature_report do
  print_rows.(signature_report["rows"])
  IO.puts("")
  print_label.(signature_report)
else
  IO.puts(no_derive.(report))
end

:ok
```

### A declared value changed and signed again

Each of the four `declared-*` files this cell can run is a sound proof with one declared
value changed, which the generator then signed again with the test key. `SPEC.md` section
13.3 calls such a file a single-defect case: it changes a sound proof, adjusts every other
value so that no other check fails, and signs the result again with its key. For these
four, its Declared values class says the changed value fails `derivation_mismatch` at its
own pointer.

This cell runs the one the edit cell picked for the proof's type:
`declared-item_hash` for an item, `declared-block_hash-block` for a block,
`declared-observation_hash-observation-nist` for a beacon and
`declared-observation_hash-subject` for `entropy_nist`. For any other type it takes
`declared-item_hash`. It runs the verifier on the file with `run_file`, under the
configuration its case in `vectors/cases.json` gives: environment `test` and the keyring
`vectors/keyring.test.json`.

The file passes the gate, SHAPE and POLICY. DERIVE fails at the changed value, and the
failing trace row gives the declared value beside the recomputed one, which is the value
the sound proof declares. A failure at DERIVE does not end the run, but it closes the
external gate (section 10.2): CHAIN and SOURCES give `skip prior_failure`, WINDOW gives
`skip prior_failure` (section 11.3), and the label is `failed`.

```elixir
vector_file = Path.join([repository, "vectors", "negative", vector_name <> ".proof.json"])

vector_config =
  Truestamp.Verifier.Config.new!(
    environment: "test",
    keyring: {:file, Path.join(repository, "vectors/keyring.test.json")}
  )

{:ok, vector_run} = Truestamp.Verifier.run_file(vector_config, vector_file)
vector_report = Truestamp.Verifier.Report.report(vector_run)

IO.puts("Proof        #{Path.relative_to(vector_file, repository)}")
IO.puts("Type         #{vector_report["type"]}")
IO.puts("")
IO.puts("The failing trace rows:")

vector_run.trace
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.filter(fn {row, _number} -> row["status"] == "fail" end)
|> Enum.each(fn {row, number} -> print_trace_row.(row, number) end)

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("ENVIRONMENT to SHAPE, the failing DERIVE rows, and every step after DERIVE:")

print_rows.(
  rows_of.(vector_report, ~w(ENVIRONMENT KEY_BINDING SIGNATURE SHAPE)) ++
    Enum.filter(rows_of.(vector_report, ~w(DERIVE)), &(&1["status"] == "fail")) ++
    rows_of.(vector_report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES WINDOW EXPECTED_HASH))
)

IO.puts("")
print_label.(vector_report)

:ok
